Trace:

  1. convertToAssets only takes the escrow branch when t_CurrentAccount != address(0) and _isWithdrawRequestPending →
  BaseLPLib.hasPendingWithdrawals sees a non-zero requestId for some pool token. That much of the claim is literally
  true.
  2. But the claimed trigger is "call __initiateWithdraw with a dust yieldTokenAmount while sharesHeld is the full
  balance". __initiateWithdraw is internal and override — it is invoked by the base AbstractYieldStrategy entry point,
  which supplies both yieldTokenAmount and sharesHeld. Neither the file under review nor the derived CurveConvex2Token
  contains that external entry point, so nothing shown lets an unprivileged caller decouple the two arguments. The
  natural reading of the signature (the strategy converting the account's shares into the corresponding yield-token
  amount) is that they are consistent by construction; the claim needs a divergence that exists in neither file. Under
  the tie-break that alone is fatal.
  3. Even granting an attacker-chosen dust amount, the economics run the wrong way. BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw records
  sharesAmount: sharesHeld alongside yieldTokenAmount: exitBalances[i]. The escrow valuation getWithdrawRequestValue
  asks each manager to value shares against that recorded pair, i.e. dust yield tokens spread across the full share
  count. The result is a drastic undervaluation of the attacker's own position, not an inflated one — and on redemption
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest computes yieldTokensBurned = w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem / w.sharesAmount, so
  burning the full shares returns only the dust proceeds. The remaining staked LP is not returned by the escrow path at
  all. That is pure self-harm, not profit.
  4. getWithdrawRequestValue additionally does require(hasRequest) for every token in TOKENS() (two, for the derived
  Curve pool). initiateWithdraw skips any token with exitBalances[i] == 0, so a dust proportional exit that rounds one
  leg to zero leaves that token with no request and makes the escrow valuation revert outright — a bricked position for
  the attacker, not a manipulated price. And manager.initiateWithdraw "will revert if there is already a pending
  withdraw", so the state cannot be re-timed or refreshed.
  5. No path is shown by which the mispriced position benefits the attacker against other holders: convertToAssets on
  the escrow branch is gated on t_CurrentAccount being that account, so the distortion applies to the attacker's own
  valuation, and it is a discount rather than a premium.

  The claim identifies a size-agnostic boolean but never establishes an unprivileged control point for the dust amount,
  nor a direction of error that profits the attacker.

